    <string name="about_already_unlocked">The concert is already on. Find Opus mode in Settings › Appearance.</string>

    <string name="about_how_title">How it works</string>
    <string name="about_how_clock_title">One clock to track</string>
    <string name="about_how_clock_body">The plan follows your body clock as a single time: the low point of your core temperature, a few hours before you usually wake. Your sleep times and chronotype set where it starts.</string>
    <string name="about_how_light_title">Light does the heavy lifting</string>
    <string name="about_how_light_body">Light in the hours after that low point moves your clock earlier; light in the hours before it moves it later. That\'s why the plan sometimes asks for sunglasses at noon.</string>
    <string name="about_how_pace_title">A realistic pace</string>
    <string name="about_how_pace_body">On Balanced and Max, the plan moves your clock up to 1 hour a day earlier before you fly and 1.5 hours a day after you land. Moving later is easier: up to 1.5 hours a day before and 2 after. Gentle goes slower. Going east, it may choose the long way round if that\'s kinder.</string>
    <string name="about_how_extras_title">Sleep, caffeine and melatonin</string>
    <string name="about_how_extras_body">Sleep moves with your clock. Caffeine keeps you sharp in the body\'s night, with a cut-off well before bed. Melatonin, if you switch it on, is a small 0.5 mg dose, and only for shifting earlier.</string>
    <string name="about_how_check_title">Checked against a model</string>
    <string name="about_how_check_body">Plans that shift your clock are run through a published mathematical model of the human body clock (Hannay, Booth &amp; Forger, 2019). It estimates how many days you\'ll take to adapt, with the plan and without it.</string>
    <string name="about_refs_title">Built on</string>
    <string name="about_ref_1">Eastman &amp; Burgess (2009). How to travel the world without jet lag.</string>
    <string name="about_ref_2">Khalsa et al. (2003); St Hilaire et al. (2012). Human phase response curves to light.</string>
    <string name="about_ref_3">Burgess et al. (2008, 2010). Phase response curves to melatonin.</string>
    <string name="about_ref_4">Roenneberg et al. (2003, 2007). Chronotype and mid-sleep on free days.</string>
    <string name="about_ref_5">Forger, Jewett &amp; Kronauer (1999); Hannay, Booth &amp; Forger (2019). Models of the circadian pacemaker.</string>
    <string name="about_ref_6">Czeisler et al. (1999). The human body clock runs at about 24.2 hours.</string>

    <string name="about_disclaimer_title">Not medical advice</string>
    <string name="about_disclaimer_body">Clockblock gives general information about timing light, sleep and caffeine. It isn\'t a diagnosis or treatment, and it\'s meant for healthy adults, not flight crew on duty.</string>
    <string name="about_disclaimer_melatonin">Melatonin is a medicine in many countries. Talk to a doctor or pharmacist first, especially if you are pregnant, take other medicines or have a long-term condition.</string>
    <string name="about_disclaimer_drive">Don\'t drive when you\'re short on sleep, whatever the plan says.</string>

